//
// A NULLABLE IS LAID OUT AND OWNED LIKE ITS DENSE TWIN (`@FR-L-Null`: `layout(τ) = layout(τ?)`).
// Making a value nullable changes what its bytes mean, never where they are: a nullable collection
// lays out like the dense one, a `S?` tuple element owns its store as an `S` element does (a text
// element is a stack `Str`, loft#1004), and a
// payload field of a nullable struct enum resolves like a struct field.  Three sections, each with
// its cells and receipt.
//
// ── a-nullable-collection-lays-out-like-its-dense-twin ──
//
// @FR-L-Null — `layout(τ) = layout(τ?)`.  Making a collection nullable changes what the
// bytes MEAN and never where they are, so a `τ?` has to reach every layout decision by the
// same route its dense twin does.
//
// Three sites read the shape BARE and so never saw the wrapper (loft#1125):
//
//   * the pre-registration walk that gives an `index` its bookkeeping triple a layout.  It
//     runs at the end of `fill_all` precisely so `#left_N / #right_N / #color_N` are
//     appended to the ELEMENT struct before the type is sized; `Optional(Index(…))` matched
//     nothing, the triple was appended afterwards, and all three kept `position: 0` — on
//     top of each other and of the first real field.  The nullable form then refused to lay
//     out at all while the dense one was fine.
//   * the `null` → sentinel conversion, which asked for `Type::Vector` alone and was short
//     by the five KEYED kinds and by the wrapper.  A `spatial<P[x,y]>? = null` local kept a
//     bare `Value::Null`, which writes nothing, so the scope-exit `OpFreeRef` read the
//     untouched bytes as store #0 and refused to free the STACK (BUG #306).
//   * the OMITTED nullable collection FIELD, which took the zero its type gives — and zero
//     IS the empty collection, the one value absence has to be distinguishable from
//     (loft#917).  `c: vector<τ>? = null` therefore read back present-and-empty.
//
// ⚠ Each keyed kind gets its OWN element struct here, and that is the load-bearing part of
// the arrangement rather than tidiness.  The layout defect is invisible whenever the same
// index type ALSO has a dense local somewhere in the program: that one registers the
// bookkeeping in time and the nullable form inherits a correct layout.  A guard that put a
// dense sibling beside every nullable cell — which is how the shape was first written down
// — passes on the broken build.
//
// @falsified-at: 80b17d25 — the file does not COMPILE there: `type layout: NL125i: fields
// '#left_1' [@0..4) and '#right_1' [@0..) overlap` on both backends, exit 1 with zero
// assertions run.  The three narrower cells were measured separately on that build: the
// spatial local halts with BUG #306 on `--interpret` (native answers), and every omitted
// nullable-collection field answers `false` to `== null` on both.
//   HOLDS: assertions, leak, panic, free-refusal, expectations are equal on both trees.
//   Falsify prints only the channels that MOVE and requires HERE to be clean, so each of
//   those is clean on both. The native side was not scored in this sweep.
//
// ── a-nullable-tuple-element-owns-like-its-dense-twin ──
//
// A tuple element declared `S?` owns its store exactly as a `S` element does: the value is
// the element's, and the store is released when the tuple's frame dies.
//
// `owned_elements` answers which of a tuple's elements hold something to free.  It is the
// membership question `is_dbref` already owns, and it was a hand-spelled copy — so it read
// the element type BARE, and `Optional(τ)` fell to the catch-all.  `Optional(τ)` occupies
// τ's slot exactly (@FR-L-Null: layout(τ) = layout(τ?)), so a `(integer, S?)` element holds
// the same `DbRef` as `(integer, S)` and owns the same store; unrecognised, it got no
// `OpFreeRef` and leaked one record per evaluation, unbounded in a loop.
//
// ⚠ The corpus HAD four programs with a nullable tuple element and none of them could see
// this: every one of them pinned the element to `text?`, `integer?` or a type variable, and
// `text` is the one owning shape `tuple_owned_elem_frees` skips ON PURPOSE (a tuple's text
// element is a stack `Str`, not a store — loft#1004).  The store-backed cell is the one
// nobody wrote.  Each cell below is written beside its non-null twin so the expected value
// never has to be derived by hand.
//
// @falsified-at: 04482989 — measured BY HAND against a pristine worktree, because the leak
// channel is what moves and `falsify.sh` reads that off stderr, which a `main`-less guard
// never prints.  A `main`-ful mirror of this file leaks `SNTup×30` at 04482989 and is clean
// here, on BOTH backends; every assertion above passes on both trees.
//   ⚠ That control is on no public ref, so this receipt records a falsification that
//   cannot be re-run — see GUARDS.md § The patch receipt for the form that can be.
//   ARMED: `LOFT_STRICT_STORES=1`. This file has no `main`, so falsify routes it through
//   --tests, where the leak column is BLIND — an unarmed run reports `leak none` on BOTH
//   trees whatever it leaks, which records a leak guard as INERT instead of failing loudly.
//   GUARDS.md § A guard that never failed is not a guard.
//
// ── a-nullable-struct-enum-payload-field-resolves-like-a-struct-field ──
//
// `Sh?` is `Optional(Enum(Sh, true))` — the same record behind a nullability marker
// (`@FR-L-Null`) — so a payload field named by every variant is reached through a nullable
// receiver exactly as a struct's field is through `s: S?` (C89: payloads are named fields
// read straight).
//
// Found walking `@FR-B-Copy`: the field resolver's struct-enum branch matched the receiver
// type BARE while `type_elm` beside it peels the nullable for the struct path.  A READ
// reported "Unknown field Sh.n"; a WRITE re-typed the receiver on pass 1 ("Variable 'e'
// cannot change type from Sh? to integer") — two messages, one root, and neither named the
// `?` that was the axis.  `s.v` on an `s: S?` resolved all along.
//
// @falsified-at: 12e58a4d — interpret exit 1 -> 0, native exit 1 -> 0.  The channel is the
//   REFUSAL: on that build the file does not compile ("cannot change type from NeSh? to
//   integer"), so no assertion ever ran.
//   HOLDS: on --interpret, assertions, leak, panic, free-refusal, expectations are equal on
//   both trees, measured by `scripts/falsify.sh --bulk` against 12e58a4d; falsify prints only
//   the channels that MOVE, and requires HERE to be clean, so each of those is clean on both.
//   The exit channel carries this one on that backend. The native side was not scored in that
//   run.

struct NL125i { k: integer = 0, v: integer = 0 }
struct NL125h { k: integer = 0, v: integer = 0 }
struct NL125s { k: integer = 0, v: integer = 0 }
struct NL125t { key: text = "", v: integer = 0 }
struct NL125p { x: integer = 0, y: integer = 0, v: integer = 0 }
struct NL125v { v: integer = 0 }

// One nullable local per kind, with NO dense sibling of the same element type anywhere in
// this file — the axis the defect lives on.
fn only_null_index()   -> boolean { c: index<NL125i[k]>?      = null; c == null }
fn only_null_hash()    -> boolean { c: hash<NL125h[k]>?       = null; c == null }
fn only_null_sorted()  -> boolean { c: sorted<NL125s[k]>?     = null; c == null }
fn only_null_trie()    -> boolean { c: trie<NL125t[key]>?     = null; c == null }
fn only_null_spatial() -> boolean { c: spatial<NL125p[x,y]>?  = null; c == null }
fn only_null_vector()  -> boolean { c: vector<NL125v>?        = null; c == null }

// The nullable local BESIDE a dense one of the same type: the dense form must still work,
// and the nullable local must still be released without touching the stack store.
struct NL125b { k: integer = 0, v: integer = 0 }
fn null_beside_dense() -> integer {
  absent: index<NL125b[k]>? = null;
  present: index<NL125b[k]> = [NL125b { k: 1, v: 11 }];
  if absent == null { present[1].v } else { -1 }
}

struct NL125f { k: integer = 0, v: integer = 0 }
struct HoldIdx  { c: index<NL125f[k]>?  = null, n: integer = 0 }
struct HoldHash { c: hash<NL125f[k]>?   = null, n: integer = 0 }
struct HoldVec  { c: vector<integer>?   = null, n: integer = 0 }
struct HoldBare { c: vector<integer>?,          n: integer = 0 }
struct HoldFull { c: vector<integer>    = [4, 5], n: integer = 0 }

fn test_a_nullable_collection_local_is_the_only_registration_it_needs() {
  // Reaching these at all is most of the cell: the layout defect refused the FILE.
  assert(only_null_index(), "a nullable index local");
  assert(only_null_hash(), "a nullable hash local");
  assert(only_null_sorted(), "a nullable sorted local");
  assert(only_null_trie(), "a nullable trie local");
  assert(only_null_spatial(), "a nullable spatial local");
  assert(only_null_vector(), "a nullable vector local");
}

fn test_the_dense_twin_beside_it_is_unchanged() {
  assert(null_beside_dense() == 11, "the dense index still reads, got {null_beside_dense()}");
}

fn test_an_omitted_nullable_collection_field_is_absent_not_empty() {
  a = HoldIdx { n: 3 };
  assert(a.c == null, "an omitted `index<τ[k]>? = null` field is absent");
  assert(a.n == 3, "and the sibling field is intact, got {a.n}");
  b = HoldHash { n: 3 };
  assert(b.c == null, "an omitted `hash<τ[k]>? = null` field is absent");
  c = HoldVec { n: 3 };
  assert(c.c == null, "an omitted `vector<τ>? = null` field is absent");
  // No declared default at all: a nullable field's zero is its null, the same answer
  // `text?` / `integer?` / `S?` already give.
  d = HoldBare { n: 3 };
  assert(d.c == null, "an omitted nullable field with no declared default is absent");
}

fn test_the_spellings_that_are_not_absence_still_are_not() {
  // `[]` is EMPTY, and empty is the value absence has to be distinguishable from.
  a = HoldVec { c: [], n: 3 };
  assert(!(a.c == null), "an explicit `[]` is not absent");
  assert(len(a.c ?? [7]) == 0, "and it is empty, got {len(a.c ?? [7])}");
  b = HoldVec { c: [8, 9], n: 3 };
  assert(!(b.c == null), "a filled field is not absent");
  assert(len(b.c ?? []) == 2, "with its elements, got {len(b.c ?? [])}");
  // A NON-nullable collection field with a declared default keeps it.
  c = HoldFull { n: 3 };
  assert(len(c.c) == 2, "a non-null field keeps its declared default, got {len(c.c)}");
  assert(c.c[1] == 5, "including its elements, got {c.c[1]}");
}


struct SNTup { a: integer = 0 }

fn mk_tup(n: integer) -> SNTup { SNTup { a: n } }

fn opt_second(n: integer) -> integer { t: (integer, SNTup?) = (n, mk_tup(n)); t.0 + (t.1 ?? SNTup {}).a }
fn dense_second(n: integer) -> integer { t: (integer, SNTup) = (n, mk_tup(n)); t.0 + t.1.a }

fn opt_first(n: integer) -> integer { t: (SNTup?, integer) = (mk_tup(n), n); t.1 + (t.0 ?? SNTup {}).a }
fn dense_first(n: integer) -> integer { t: (SNTup, integer) = (mk_tup(n), n); t.1 + t.0.a }

fn opt_absent(n: integer) -> integer { t: (integer, SNTup?) = (n, null); t.0 + (t.1 ?? SNTup {}).a }

fn opt_text(n: integer) -> integer { t: (integer, text?) = (n, "ab"); t.0 + len(t.1 ?? "") }
fn opt_int(n: integer) -> integer { t: (integer, integer?) = (n, n); t.0 + (t.1 ?? 0) }
fn opt_vector(n: integer) -> integer { t: (integer, vector<integer>?) = (n, [n, n]); t.0 + len(t.1 ?? []) }

fn test_a_nullable_element_answers_what_its_dense_twin_answers() {
  for n in 0..4 {
    assert(opt_second(n) == dense_second(n), "second element at n={n}: {opt_second(n)} vs {dense_second(n)}");
    assert(opt_first(n) == dense_first(n), "first element at n={n}: {opt_first(n)} vs {dense_first(n)}");
  }
  assert(opt_second(3) == 6, "the value itself, got {opt_second(3)}");
}

fn test_an_absent_element_takes_the_default() {
  assert(opt_absent(3) == 3, "an absent element contributes its default, got {opt_absent(3)}");
}

fn test_the_other_element_kinds_are_unchanged() {
  // `text?` is the deliberate skip and `integer?` is a scalar — neither is store-backed, and
  // both are here so a widening of the membership test has to keep them where they are.
  assert(opt_text(3) == 5, "a nullable text element, got {opt_text(3)}");
  assert(opt_int(3) == 6, "a nullable integer element, got {opt_int(3)}");
  assert(opt_vector(3) == 5, "a nullable vector element, got {opt_vector(3)}");
}

fn test_it_does_not_scale_with_the_evaluation_count() {
  // The store half.  Each call mints one record through a nullable element; the leak gate
  // reads the count, so the loop is what turns one retained record into a visible failure.
  t = 0;
  for n in 0..6 { t += opt_second(n) + opt_first(n); }
  assert(t == 60, "six evaluations of each shape, got {t}");
}

struct NeIn { v: integer }
enum NeSh { NeCi { n: integer }, NeSq { n: integer } }

fn test_a_payload_field_reads_through_a_nullable_receiver() {
  e: NeSh? = NeCi { n: 1 };
  x = e.n;
  assert(x == 1, "a payload read through a nullable receiver answered {x}");
}

fn test_a_payload_field_writes_through_a_nullable_receiver() {
  e: NeSh? = NeCi { n: 1 };
  e.n = 9;
  assert((e ?? NeSq { n: -1 }).n == 9, "a payload write through a nullable receiver was lost");
}

fn test_a_write_after_a_guard_lands() {
  e: NeSh? = NeCi { n: 1 };
  if e != null {
    e.n = 9;
  }
  assert((e ?? NeSq { n: -1 }).n == 9, "a guarded payload write was lost");
}

// The struct twin, so a divergence between the two reads as such.
fn test_the_struct_twin_reads_and_writes_the_same_way() {
  s: NeIn? = NeIn { v: 1 };
  x = s.v;
  s.v = 9;
  assert(x == 1 && (s ?? NeIn { v: 0 }).v == 9, "the nullable struct twin moved");
}

// And the bind beside it: a copy taken from a nullable struct-enum, then the source's
// payload written through the nullable spelling — the two fixes meet here.
fn test_a_copy_survives_a_payload_write_through_the_nullable_source() {
  e: NeSh? = NeCi { n: 1 };
  c = e;
  e.n = 9;
  assert((c ?? NeSq { n: -1 }).n == 1, "the copy read the source's later write");
}
